1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
One of the most important things we need to verify is whether an extremely long subtitle can automatically fit inside the available screen area without becoming unreadable.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
When a subtitle contains many words, the rendering system should intelligently divide the sentence into multiple balanced lines instead of producing one enormous horizontal line.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,000
The spacing between the first line and the second line should remain visually consistent regardless of how many characters are contained inside each individual line.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
A very long sentence should never cause the subtitle container to move outside the designated safe area near the bottom portion of the video frame.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
If the text cannot fit at the default font size, the engine should reduce its size according to a predictable rule instead of clipping characters on either side.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Automatic line wrapping should preferably break the sentence around natural word boundaries instead of dividing a single word into two visually confusing fragments.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
We should also verify that punctuation marks such as commas, periods, apostrophes, question marks, and quotation marks remain attached to the correct surrounding words.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Another important scenario occurs when one extremely long subtitle is immediately followed by another subtitle with a completely different number of characters.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Rendering the same long subtitle repeatedly should generate exactly the same pixels every time, proving that text measurement and layout calculations are deterministic.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Finally, this deliberately oversized sentence verifies that the renderer continues to respect safe areas, maximum width, line spacing, alignment, font sizing, and clipping rules at the same time.
